                                             *** Getting Started


Congratulations! If you are able to read this file, you apparently managed to download and extract the CheckMATE tarball! Now there is only a couple of steps which need to be done in order to install CheckMATE.

CheckMATE uses the standard ./configure + make + make install chain. For the configuration step, you must not provide --prefix (it will screw up the installation) but you have to provide the following parameters:
--with-rootsys=(ROOTDIR)
--with-delphes=(DELPHESDIR)

If desired, event generation can be enabled by furthermore providing the following optional parameters
--with-madgraph=(MG5MCNLODIR)
--with-pythia=(PYTHIADIR)

For installation in a remote directory (by default the installation is in the source directory):
--prefix=(DIR)  - defines the target directory
--with-resultsdir=(RESULTSDIR)   - when using --prefix this defines the standard output directory (for each run one can override this later); if none defined CheckMATE will output to prefix/share/results
The results folder can also be defined using the environmental variable CHECKMATE_RESULTS

A) Steps for installation in the current folder:
    1) autoreconf
    2) ./configure --with-delphes=... --with-python=... --with-pythia=... etc.
    3) make (you can add -j8 or similar to speed things up for multicore making)
    4) you can check the installation by running a full analysis (requires MadGraph and Pythia):
        a) ./bin/CheckMATE ./bin/13tev_test.in    (quicker)
        b) ./bin/CheckMATE ./bin/13tev_testfull2.in   (takes time)
        c) using a hepmc file (no MadGraph or Pythia needed; note that you might need to update the file path in the input file):
            curl -L "https://mycloud.fuw.edu.pl/s/xgWj6oaTMw2FCY3/download" --output "tag_1_pythia8_events.hepmc.tar.gz"
            tar -xvf tag_1_pythia8_events.hepmc.tar.gz
            ./bin/CheckMATE ./bin/13tev_test_hepmc.in
        
B) Steps for installation in a remote folder (defined using --prefix= ):
    1) autoreconf
    2) ./configure --prefix=your_folder --with-resultsdir=... --with-delphes=... --with-python=... --with-pythia=... etc.
    3) make (you can add -j8 or similar to speed things up for multicore making)
    4) you can check the installation by running a full analysis (requires MadGraph and Pythia):
        a) your_folder/bin/CheckMATE your_folder/share/CheckMATE/bin/13tev_test.in    (quicker)
        b) your_folder/bin/CheckMATE your_folder/bin/13tev_testfull2.in   (takes time)     
    5) The results folder can be defined using the environment variable CHECKMATE_RESULTS
    6) There is a convenience script for downloading of data after build, download.sh.

If you are not an expert on this subject, we highly recommend to go to the 'Installation Tutorial' on our website, http://checkmate.hepforge.org . There we will go through the above configuration step in all detail and most importantly explain to you how to find and install missing libraries. 
